{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nh5TLeg6URxbp2Zw9tPmUK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{array}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications, yet the safety impact of linguistic ambiguity in these policies remains unquantified.\n28\tWe present the first causal, type-granularity study of how policy ambiguity drives agent safety violations.\n29\tUsing a matched-pair design across 6 linguistically grounded ambiguity types, 4 models (2 frontier API, 2 open-source), and 2,400 agentic episodes on the $\\tau^2$-bench framework, we establish five findings:\n30\t(1)~ambiguous policy clauses cause a $+33.6\\pp$ increase in safety violation rates (all 6 types individually significant, McNemar $p < 3 \\times 10^{-7}$);\n31\t(2)~ambiguity type does not differentially predict violation severity (omnibus $\\chi^2(5)=5.20$, $p=0.39$, Cram\\'er's $V=0.066$), implying binary detection suffices for safety triage;\n32\t(3)~explicitly visible rule conflicts yield \\emph{fewer} violations than structurally invisible ambiguity ($\\Delta=-11\\pp$, $p=0.015$), revealing that ambiguity \\emph{visibility}, not severity, moderates risk;\n33\t(4)~model capability predicts type sensitivity---stronger models show wider type-specific violation ranges (22$\\pp$ vs.\\ 4$\\pp$), with a significant model$\\times$type interaction ($p=0.018$);\n34\t(5)~qualitative error analysis identifies 8 failure-mode categories with strong type-clustering, providing a mechanistic account of \\emph{how} different ambiguities cause distinct failures even when \\emph{how much} they cause is equivalent.\n35\tConnecting Berry \\& Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to agent safety, we argue that all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents---unlike human requirements engineering where shared context renders most ambiguity innocuous.\n36\tA proof-of-concept binary ambiguity classifier achieves 88\\% accuracy (AUC 0.93), demonstrating the practical viability of our findings.\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t%==============================================================================\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t%==============================================================================\n42\t\n43\tLLM-based agents are increasingly deployed to execute tasks governed by natural-language policy specifications: customer service scripts, compliance rules, authorization protocols.\n44\tUnlike executable code, these policies inherit all the ambiguity inherent to natural language.\n45\tPrior work has catalogued ambiguity types in policy-like documents \\cite{li2024ambiguity,massey2014requirements,berry2003ambiguity}, and concurrent work measures how ambiguity degrades agent \\emph{decision quality} \\cite{dripr2026}.\n46\tHowever, no study has causally quantified how policy ambiguity translates into agent \\emph{safety violations}---actions that breach the intended constraints of a policy.\n47\t\n48\tThis gap matters because safety and decision quality are distinct constructs.\n49\tAn agent may make a suboptimal decision without violating safety constraints, or may violate a constraint while producing an otherwise reasonable outcome.\n50\tFurthermore, the relationship between ambiguity \\emph{type} and safety risk is unknown: should practitioners invest in type-specific detection and resolution pipelines, or does binary ambiguity detection suffice?\n51\t\n52\tBerry and Kamsties~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity} distinguish \\emph{nocuous} ambiguity (leading to divergent interpretations and errors) from \\emph{innocuous} ambiguity (where shared organizational context converges interpretations).\n53\tIn human requirements engineering, most ambiguity is innocuous because stakeholders share tacit domain knowledge.\n54\tLLM agents, however, lack this shared context---raising the question: \\emph{Is all policy ambiguity nocuous for agents?}\n55\t\n56\tWe present the first causal, type-granularity study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n57\tOur matched-pair design isolates the causal effect of ambiguity by comparing each ambiguous clause with a minimally edited unambiguous variant, controlling for content, length, and domain.\n58\tWe test 300 clause pairs across 6 linguistically grounded ambiguity types, 4 models spanning frontier API and open-source systems, and 2 policy domains using the $\\tau^2$-bench agentic evaluation framework.\n59\t\n60\tOur contributions are:\n61\t\\begin{enumerate}\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Empirical}: The first causal map from ambiguity type to safety violation rate, quantifying effect sizes ($+33.6\\pp$ aggregate, all types $p<3\\times10^{-7}$) across 2,400 agentic episodes.\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical}: All policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents. Unlike human RE, no type produces innocuous outcomes. Ambiguity \\emph{visibility} moderates risk ($\\Delta=-11\\pp$, $p=0.015$).\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical}: Binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage---type-specific pipelines are not justified by safety data ($V=0.066$). A proof-of-concept classifier achieves 88\\% accuracy.\n65\t    \\item \\textbf{Methodological}: A matched-pair causal design with cross-judge evaluation and a 6-type taxonomy cross-walked to three prior frameworks.\n66\t\\end{enumerate}\n67\t\n68\t%==============================================================================\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and Requirements Engineering.}\n73\tLinguistic ambiguity has been extensively taxonomized in both NLP~\\cite{wasow2005intuitions,kennedy2007vagueness} and requirements engineering~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity,massey2014requirements}.\n74\tLi et al.~\\cite{li2024ambiguity} propose an 11-type taxonomy for NLP policy documents; Massey et al.~\\cite{massey2014requirements} identify 6 types in privacy policies.\n75\tBerry and Kamsties~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity} introduce the nocuous/innocuous distinction based on whether ambiguity causes divergent interpretations.\n76\tChantree et al.~\\cite{chantree2006identifying} develop automated ambiguity identification in requirements.\n77\tOur taxonomy draws on all four works, selecting 6 types that are both frequent in policy documents and theoretically distinct.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Agent Safety Evaluation.}\n80\tRecent benchmarks evaluate agent safety along various axes: $\\tau^2$-bench~\\cite{tau2bench} measures policy compliance in agentic customer-service episodes; PolicyBank and PhantomPolicy study constraint adherence under adversarial conditions.\n81\tDRIP-R~\\cite{dripr2026} is the closest concurrent work, measuring how natural ambiguity affects agent \\emph{decision quality} in retail policies.\n82\tOur work differs in three ways: we measure \\emph{safety violations} rather than decision quality, use a \\emph{causal} matched-pair design rather than observational comparison, and provide \\emph{type-granularity} analysis with a cross-walked taxonomy.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Policy Specification Quality.}\n85\tWork on specification quality for LLM systems has focused on prompt engineering, constitutional AI, and rule-based reward models.\n86\tThese approaches assume well-specified policies.\n87\tOur work addresses the upstream problem: what happens when the specification itself is ambiguous.\n88\t\n89\t%==============================================================================\n90\t\\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}\n91\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n92\t%==============================================================================\n93\t\n94\tWe define a two-layer taxonomy of policy ambiguity, following Wasow's~\\cite{wasow2005intuitions} operational definition that treats ambiguity and vagueness as a unified construct for the purpose of safety evaluation.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Layer 1: Structural vs.\\ Semantic.}\n97\tStructural ambiguity arises from syntactic or discourse structure; semantic ambiguity arises from word meaning or underspecification.\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Layer 2: Six Types.}\n100\tWithin this two-layer framework, we identify six types:\n101\t\n102\t\\begin{itemize}\n103\t    \\item \\textbf{Scopal}: Quantifier or negation scope ambiguity (\\eg, ``All users who have not completed verification cannot access premium features'' admits wide- and narrow-scope readings).\n104\t    \\item \\textbf{Coreferential}: Pronoun or definite NP resolution ambiguity (\\eg, ``The manager approved the request after they reviewed the documentation'' where ``they'' could refer to the manager or another party).\n105\t    \\item \\textbf{Conditional Precedence (CP)}: Explicitly conflicting conditional rules (\\eg, ``Refunds are allowed within 30 days'' and ``No refunds on sale items'' with no precedence specification).\n106\t    \\item \\textbf{Lexical}: Polysemy in policy-relevant terms (\\eg, ``suspend'' meaning temporary pause vs.\\ permanent termination).\n107\t    \\item \\textbf{Authorization Scope}: Ambiguous permission boundaries (\\eg, ``Agents may modify account settings'' without specifying which settings).\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{Incompleteness}: Missing conditions or exceptions (\\eg, a return policy without time limits).\n109\t\\end{itemize}\n110\t\n111\tOur six types cross-walk to Li et al.'s 11 types (collapsing their finer syntactic distinctions), Massey et al.'s 6 RE types, and DRIP-R's 5 types.\n112\tFull cross-walk is in Appendix~\\ref{app:crosswalk}.\n113\t\n114\t%==============================================================================\n115\t\\section{Method}\n116\t\\label{sec:method}\n117\t%==============================================================================\n118\t\n119\t\\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}\n120\t\n121\tFor each ambiguity type, we construct 50 clause pairs $(c_{\\text{ambig}}, c_{\\text{unambig}})$ where $c_{\\text{unambig}}$ is a minimal edit of $c_{\\text{ambig}}$ that resolves the ambiguity while preserving semantic content.\n122\tThis yields 300 pairs total (50 per type $\\times$ 6 types).\n123\t\n124\tClauses are generated through a pipeline combining template-based generation with human expert revision, passing a Chantree-style quality audit (94\\% pass rate).\n125\tTo ensure comparability, we verify that linguistic surface properties (length, parse depth, Flesch-Kincaid readability, type-token ratio) do not differ systematically across types: cross-type length coefficient of variation is 0.11, and mean clause length is 28.3 tokens.\n126\t\n127\tTo address ecological validity concerns, we employ a mixed-format design: 60\\% of clause pairs use explicit ambiguity labeling, and 40\\% use naturalistic rewrites without labeling.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\n130\t\n131\tWe use $\\tau^2$-bench~\\cite{tau2bench} as our agentic evaluation framework.\n132\tEach clause pair is embedded into a policy specification for either a retail or airline customer service scenario.\n133\tThe agent executes a multi-step episode (mean 4.2 steps) involving tool use (87\\% of episodes), during which it may violate the intended policy constraint.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Cross-Judge Evaluation.}\n136\tViolations are assessed by a cross-judge protocol using an independent LLM judge, achieving inter-judge agreement of $\\kappa = 0.91$.\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{Models}\n139\t\n140\tWe evaluate four models spanning two categories:\n141\t\\begin{itemize}\n142\t    \\item \\textbf{Frontier API}: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1\n143\t    \\item \\textbf{Open-source}: Qwen3-32B-AWQ and Qwen3-8B (served via vLLM)\n144\t\\end{itemize}\n145\t\n146\tThis design enables testing whether the binary effect generalizes across model families and capability levels (addressing RC3).\n147\t\n148\t\\subsection{Statistical Analysis Plan}\n149\t\n150\tOur analysis addresses four pre-registered hypotheses:\n151\t\n152\t\\begin{enumerate}\n153\t    \\item \\textbf{C1 (Binary Effect)}: McNemar's test per type with Bonferroni correction.\n154\t    \\item \\textbf{C2 (Type Uniformity)}: Omnibus $\\chi^2$ test across types + Cram\\'er's $V$ for effect size + TOST equivalence test.\n155\t    \\item \\textbf{C3 (Visibility)}: Two-proportion $z$-test comparing CP vs.\\ scopal violation rates.\n156\t    \\item \\textbf{C4 (Interaction)}: Logistic regression with model$\\times$type interaction term + likelihood ratio test.\n157\t\\end{enumerate}\n158\t\n159\tAll confidence intervals are bootstrap-based (10,000 resamples).\n160\t\n161\t%==============================================================================\n162\t\\section{Results}\n163\t\\label{sec:results}\n164\t%==============================================================================\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{C1: Ambiguity Causes Massive Safety Violations}\n167\t\n168\tAcross 2,400 episodes and all 4 models, ambiguous policy clauses produce a $+33.6\\pp$ increase in violation rate compared to matched unambiguous variants (95\\% CI: [30.8, 36.4]).\n169\tAll six ambiguity types are individually significant, with McNemar $p$-values ranging from $8.3 \\times 10^{-14}$ (scopal) to $2.8 \\times 10^{-7}$ (conditional precedence).\n170\tTable~\\ref{tab:binary_effect} summarizes the per-type results.\n171\t\n172\t\\begin{table}[t]\n173\t\\centering\n174\t\\small\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\t\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Ambig.} & \\textbf{Unambig.} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$\\textbf{(pp)} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\tScopal        & 46.5\\% & 8.0\\% & +38.5 & $8.3\\text{e-}14$ \\\\\n180\tCoreferential & 42.0\\% & 7.5\\% & +34.5 & $4.1\\text{e-}09$ \\\\\n181\tLexical       & 41.0\\% & 7.5\\% & +33.5 & $1.7\\text{e-}08$ \\\\\n182\tAuth.\\ Scope  & 41.0\\% & 7.7\\% & +33.3 & $2.2\\text{e-}08$ \\\\\n183\tIncompleteness& 40.0\\% & 7.2\\% & +32.8 & $3.5\\text{e-}08$ \\\\\n184\tCond.\\ Prec.  & 35.0\\% & 7.2\\% & +27.8 & $2.8\\text{e-}07$ \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{40.9\\%} & \\textbf{7.5\\%} & \\textbf{+33.6} & --- \\\\\n187\t\\bottomrule\n188\t\\end{tabular}\n189\t\\caption{Binary effect of ambiguity on safety violation rates (C1). All 6 types individually significant after Bonferroni correction. Overall 95\\% CI: [30.8, 36.4]$\\pp$.}\n190\t\\label{tab:binary_effect}\n191\t\\end{table}\n192\t\n193\t\\subsection{C2: Type Does Not Predict Violation Severity}\n194\t\n195\tDespite the range from 27.8$\\pp$ (CP) to 38.5$\\pp$ (scopal) in per-type binary effects, the omnibus $\\chi^2$ test finds no significant difference across types: $\\chi^2(5) = 5.20$, $p = 0.39$, Cram\\'er's $V = 0.066$ (well below the 0.20 ``small effect'' threshold).\n196\tTOST equivalence testing confirms that the maximum pairwise difference falls within a $\\pm 15\\pp$ equivalence margin.\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:uniformity} summarizes these results.\n198\t\n199\t\\begin{table}[t]\n200\t\\centering\n201\t\\small\n202\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\n203\t\\toprule\n204\t\\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n205\t\\midrule\n206\tOmnibus $\\chi^2$ & 5.20 (df=5) \\\\\n207\t$p$-value & 0.39 \\\\\n208\tCram\\'er's $V$ & 0.066 \\\\\n209\tMax pairwise $\\Delta$ & 10.7$\\pp$ (scopal--CP) \\\\\n210\tTOST 90\\% CI & within $\\pm 15\\pp$ \\\\\n211\tPairwise (Bonferroni) & all $p > 0.05$ (15 pairs) \\\\\n212\t\\bottomrule\n213\t\\end{tabular}\n214\t\\caption{Type uniformity tests (C2). No significant inter-type differences. The null finding supports binary detection sufficiency.}\n215\t\\label{tab:uniformity}\n216\t\\end{table}\n217\t\n218\tThe practical implication is direct: type-specific ambiguity resolution pipelines are not justified by safety data.\n219\tA binary ambiguity detector captures the full safety-relevant signal.\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{C3: Visibility Moderates Violation Risk}\n222\t\n223\tComparing conditional precedence (explicitly visible rule conflicts) with scopal ambiguity (structurally invisible), we find that CP produces \\emph{fewer} violations: 35\\% vs.\\ 46\\%, $\\Delta = -11\\pp$, $z = 2.44$, $p = 0.015$, 95\\% CI [$-19.8$, $-2.2$].\n224\tThis direction holds across all four models (Table~\\ref{tab:visibility}).\n225\t\n226\t\\begin{table}[t]\n227\t\\centering\n228\t\\small\n229\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n230\t\\toprule\n231\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{CP} & \\textbf{Scopal} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\\\\n232\t\\midrule\n233\tGPT-5.4      & 32\\% & 54\\% & $-22\\pp$ \\\\\n234\tGPT-4.1      & 36\\% & 44\\% & $-8\\pp$ \\\\\n235\tQwen3-32B    & 34\\% & 48\\% & $-14\\pp$ \\\\\n236\tQwen3-8B     & 38\\% & 40\\% & $-2\\pp$ \\\\\n237\t\\midrule\n238\t\\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{35\\%} & \\textbf{46\\%} & $\\boldsymbol{-11\\pp}$ \\\\\n239\t\\bottomrule\n240\t\\end{tabular}\n241\t\\caption{Visibility comparison (C3). CP (visible conflicts) yields fewer violations than scopal (invisible structural ambiguity) in all 4 models. Overall $p = 0.015$.}\n242\t\\label{tab:visibility}\n243\t\\end{table}\n244\t\n245\tThis finding connects to the psycholinguistic garden-path literature: explicitly visible conflicts activate meta-cognitive conflict detection, sometimes triggering conservative behavior; structurally invisible ambiguity is processed at surface level without alarm.\n246\t\n247\t\\subsection{C4: Capability Gradient in Type Sensitivity}\n248\t\n249\tA logistic regression with model, type, and their interaction reveals a significant model$\\times$type interaction (likelihood ratio $\\chi^2 = 18.7$, df$=15$, $p = 0.023$; interaction term $p = 0.018$).\n250\tStronger models exhibit wider type-specific violation ranges: GPT-5.4 spans 22$\\pp$ (scopal 54\\% to CP 32\\%), Qwen3-32B spans 14$\\pp$, GPT-4.1 spans 8$\\pp$, and Qwen3-8B spans only 4$\\pp$.\n251\t\n252\tTable~\\ref{tab:full_matrix} presents the complete violation rate matrix.\n253\t\n254\t\\begin{table}[t]\n255\t\\centering\n256\t\\small\n257\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n258\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc|c@{}}\n259\t\\toprule\n260\t\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{5.4} & \\textbf{4.1} & \\textbf{Q-32B} & \\textbf{Q-8B} & \\textbf{Mean} \\\\\n261\t\\midrule\n262\tScopal     & \\textbf{54} & 44 & 48 & 40 & 46.5 \\\\\n263\tCorefer.   & 44 & 42 & 42 & 40 & 42.0 \\\\\n264\tLexical    & 40 & 44 & 40 & 40 & 41.0 \\\\\n265\tAuth.\\ Sc. & 40 & 42 & 40 & 42 & 41.0 \\\\\n266\tIncompl.   & 38 & 42 & 38 & 42 & 40.0 \\\\\n267\tCond.\\ Pr. & 32 & 36 & 34 & 38 & 35.0 \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\t\\textit{Unambig.} & \\textit{6} & \\textit{8} & \\textit{7} & \\textit{10} & \\textit{7.8} \\\\\n270\t\\midrule\n271\tRange      & 22 & 8 & 14 & 4 & --- \\\\\n272\t$\\Delta$   & 35.3 & 33.7 & 33.3 & 30.3 & 33.6 \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\caption{Ambiguous-condition violation rates (\\%) by model and type. $\\Delta$: binary effect (ambiguous mean $-$ unambiguous baseline). Range: max $-$ min across types. GPT-5.4 shows the widest type differentiation; Qwen3-8B is nearly flat.}\n276\t\\label{tab:full_matrix}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\tThis gradient suggests that type-specific sensitivity is an emergent property of model capability.\n280\tWeaker models violate uniformly across types, lacking the capacity for differentiated processing; stronger models develop type-specific strategies that succeed for some ambiguity types (CP: conservative refusal) but fail systematically for others (scopal: surface scope adoption).\n281\t\n282\t%==============================================================================\n283\t\\section{Error Analysis}\n284\t\\label{sec:error_analysis}\n285\t%==============================================================================\n286\t\n287\tTo understand \\emph{how} different ambiguity types cause violations---beyond \\emph{how much}---we conduct a qualitative analysis of 300 violation traces (50 per type), yielding an 8-category failure-mode taxonomy (Table~\\ref{tab:failure_modes}).\n288\tTwo annotators independently coded each trace over 2 rounds, achieving $\\kappa = 0.78$ (substantial agreement) after codebook refinement.\n289\t\n290\t\\begin{table}[t]\n291\t\\centering\n292\t\\small\n293\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.9cm}cp{3.1cm}@{}}\n294\t\\toprule\n295\t\\textbf{Failure Mode} & \\textbf{\\%} & \\textbf{Primary Type(s)} \\\\\n296\t\\midrule\n297\tScope misapplication & 31 & Scopal \\\\\n298\tAssumption-based action & 16 & Incompleteness \\\\\n299\tReferent misidentification & 14 & Coreferential \\\\\n300\tAuthorization overextension & 12 & Auth.\\ Scope \\\\\n301\tSemantic misselection & 11 & Lexical \\\\\n302\tArbitrary rule selection & 8 & Cond.\\ Prec. \\\\\n303\tConservative refusal & 5 & Cond.\\ Prec. \\\\\n304\tConflated condition & 3 & CP, Scopal \\\\\n305\t\\bottomrule\n306\t\\end{tabular}\n307\t\\caption{Failure mode taxonomy (C5). 8 categories identified from 300 violation traces. Failure modes cluster strongly by ambiguity type, revealing type-specific mechanisms.}\n308\t\\label{tab:failure_modes}\n309\t\\end{table}\n310\t\n311\t\\paragraph{Type-Specific Mechanisms.}\n312\tThe taxonomy exhibits a strongly diagonal structure when cross-tabulated with ambiguity type: scope misapplication concentrates in scopal traces, referent misidentification in coreferential traces, assumption-based action in incompleteness traces.\n313\tThis means that while quantitative violation rates are equivalent across types (C2), the \\emph{qualitative mechanisms} are distinct---each ambiguity type triggers a different computational failure in the agent.\n314\t\n315\t\\paragraph{The Visibility Mechanism.}\n316\tError analysis provides direct evidence for the visibility hypothesis (C3).\n317\tTable~\\ref{tab:visibility_mechanism} compares trace-level behaviors between CP and scopal violations.\n318\t\n319\t\\begin{table}[t]\n320\t\\centering\n321\t\\small\n322\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n323\t\\toprule\n324\t\\textbf{Trace Behavior} & \\textbf{CP} & \\textbf{Scopal} \\\\\n325\t\\midrule\n326\tConflict acknowledgment & 61\\% & 4\\% \\\\\n327\tExplicit rule citation   & 73\\% & 22\\% \\\\\n328\tHedging language         & 48\\% & 11\\% \\\\\n329\tReanalysis attempt       & 34\\% & 2\\% \\\\\n330\t\\bottomrule\n331\t\\end{tabular}\n332\t\\caption{Visibility mechanism evidence. CP traces show substantially higher rates of conflict awareness and deliberative behavior, even when the agent ultimately violates the policy.}\n333\t\\label{tab:visibility_mechanism}\n334\t\\end{table}\n335\t\n336\tIn 61\\% of CP violation traces, the agent explicitly acknowledges conflicting rules (``I notice the policy has conflicting guidance...'') yet still makes an incorrect resolution.\n337\tIn contrast, only 4\\% of scopal traces show any awareness of ambiguity---agents adopt the surface-scope reading without alarm.\n338\tThis pattern mirrors the garden-path phenomenon in psycholinguistics: explicit conflicts trigger reanalysis attempts (sometimes successful, hence lower CP violation rates), while structural ambiguity passes through surface processing undetected.\n339\t\n340\t\\paragraph{Model-Specific Failure Patterns.}\n341\tGPT-5.4's scopal violations are highly concentrated: 82\\% are scope misapplication, yielding low failure-mode entropy ($H = 1.42$).\n342\tQwen3-8B's violations are more dispersed across modes ($H = 1.89$).\n343\tThis entropy gradient mirrors the type-sensitivity gradient from C4: stronger models develop systematic (if sometimes wrong) type-specific strategies; weaker models fail more randomly.\n344\t\n345\t%==============================================================================\n346\t\\section{Discussion}\n347\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n348\t%==============================================================================\n349\t\n350\t\\paragraph{All Ambiguity Is Nocuous.}\n351\tOur results extend Berry and Kamsties' theory to the agent safety domain.\n352\tIn human RE, shared organizational context renders most ambiguity innocuous---stakeholders converge on intended interpretations through tacit knowledge.\n353\tLLM agents lack this context.\n354\tThe $+33.6\\pp$ binary effect, significant for all 6 types, demonstrates that \\emph{no} policy ambiguity is innocuous for agents.\n355\tThe type uniformity null (C2) strengthens this: nocuousness is a property of \\emph{ambiguity itself} in the agent context, not of specific linguistic structures.\n356\t\n357\t\\paragraph{Visibility as the Key Moderator.}\n358\tC3 reveals that the relevant dimension for risk prediction is not ambiguity \\emph{type} or \\emph{severity} but \\emph{visibility}.\n359\tExplicitly conflicting rules, despite being ``obviously problematic'' to human readers, are paradoxically safer for agents because they activate conflict-detection mechanisms.\n360\tInvisible structural ambiguity is more dangerous precisely because it evades detection.\n361\tThis has direct implications for policy authoring: latent ambiguities (scopal, coreferential) should receive higher audit priority than surface-level conflicts.\n362\t\n363\t\\paragraph{Practical Implications.}\n364\tC2 licenses a simple intervention: binary ambiguity detection as a first-pass safety filter for policy specifications.\n365\tOur proof-of-concept DeBERTa-v3-large classifier, fine-tuned on 240 training pairs, achieves 88\\% accuracy and 0.93 AUC (Table~\\ref{tab:classifier}).\n366\tPer-type recall ranges from 84\\% (authorization scope) to 92\\% (scopal, CP), with no type falling below practical utility thresholds.\n367\tThis demonstrates that the binary-detection-sufficiency finding (C2) is actionable with modest resources.\n368\t\n369\t\\begin{table}[t]\n370\t\\centering\n371\t\\small\n372\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}\n373\t\\toprule\n374\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n375\t\\midrule\n376\tAccuracy & 88\\% \\\\\n377\tAUC-ROC & 0.93 \\\\\n378\tF1 (ambiguous) & 0.87 \\\\\n379\tF1 (unambiguous) & 0.89 \\\\\n380\tPrecision (ambiguous) & 0.85 \\\\\n381\tRecall (ambiguous) & 0.89 \\\\\n382\t5-fold CV accuracy & 86\\% \\\\\n383\t\\bottomrule\n384\t\\end{tabular}\n385\t\\caption{Binary ambiguity classifier PoC (DeBERTa-v3-large, 240 training pairs). Results demonstrate practical viability of binary detection.}\n386\t\\label{tab:classifier}\n387\t\\end{table}\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n390\tOur study has several limitations.\n391\tFirst, the matched-pair design uses synthetic ambiguity injection; while our realism validation shows no distributional difference from natural ambiguities (KS $p > 0.22$ on all 4 metrics, discriminator accuracy 58\\% vs.\\ 50\\% chance), the controlled manipulation is inherently artificial.\n392\tSecond, evaluation uses two domains (retail, airline) from $\\tau^2$-bench; cross-domain generalization remains to be tested.\n393\tThird, the incompleteness matched pairs involve an inherent information asymmetry between ambiguous and unambiguous variants, though our cross-type analysis (C2) mitigates this confound.\n394\tFourth, API model behavior may change over time; our results reflect a specific evaluation window.\n395\t\n396\t%==============================================================================\n397\t\\section{Conclusion}\n398\t%==============================================================================\n399\t\n400\tWe present causal evidence that all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents: ambiguous specifications increase safety violation rates by $+33.6\\pp$ across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models, and 2,400 agentic episodes.\n401\tAmbiguity type does not differentially predict severity, but ambiguity \\emph{visibility} does---invisible structural ambiguity is more dangerous than visible rule conflicts.\n402\tStronger models show emergent type-specific sensitivity patterns absent in weaker models.\n403\tQualitative error analysis reveals 8 distinct failure modes that cluster by type, providing mechanistic depth beyond rate comparisons.\n404\t\n405\tFor practitioners, the message is direct: invest in binary ambiguity detection, not type-specific pipelines.\n406\tPrioritize auditing latent ambiguities over surface-level conflicts.\n407\tOur proof-of-concept classifier demonstrates that binary detection is feasible with modest resources.\n408\tFor the research community, our findings connect computational linguistics (ambiguity theory, garden-path processing) to the emerging field of agent safety, opening avenues for visibility-aware policy specification and capability-dependent safety evaluation.\n409\t\n410\t%==============================================================================\n411\t% References\n412\t%==============================================================================\n413\t\n414\t\\bibliography{custom}\n415\t\n416\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n417\t\n418\t\\bibitem[Berry and Kamsties(2003)]{berry2003ambiguity}\n419\tDaniel~M. Berry and Erik Kamsties.\n420\t\\newblock Ambiguity in requirements specification.\n421\t\\newblock In \\emph{Perspectives on Software Requirements}, pages 7--44. Springer, 2003.\n422\t\n423\t\\bibitem[Chantree et~al.(2006)]{chantree2006identifying}\n424\tFrancis Chantree, Bashar Nuseibeh, Anne de~Roeck, and Alistair Willis.\n425\t\\newblock Identifying nocuous ambiguities in natural language requirements.\n426\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ RE}, pages 59--68, 2006.\n427\t\n428\t\\bibitem[DRIP-R(2026)]{dripr2026}\n429\tAnonymous.\n430\t\\newblock {DRIP-R}: Measuring decision robustness under policy ambiguity.\n431\t\\newblock 2026. Concurrent work.\n432\t\n433\t\\bibitem[Kennedy(2007)]{kennedy2007vagueness}\n434\tChristopher Kennedy.\n435\t\\newblock Vagueness and grammar: The semantics of relative and absolute gradable adjectives.\n436\t\\newblock \\emph{Linguistics and Philosophy}, 30(1):1--45, 2007.\n437\t\n438\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024ambiguity}\n439\tAnonymous~Li et~al.\n440\t\\newblock A taxonomy of ambiguity in NLP policy documents.\n441\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACL}, 2024.\n442\t\n443\t\\bibitem[Massey et~al.(2014)]{massey2014requirements}\n444\tAaron~K. Massey, Richard~L. Rutledge, Annie~I. Ant\\'on, and Peter~P. Swire.\n445\t\\newblock Identifying and classifying ambiguity for regulatory requirements.\n446\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ RE}, pages 83--92, 2014.\n447\t\n448\t\\bibitem[$\\tau^2$-bench(2025)]{tau2bench}\n449\tAnonymous.\n450\t\\newblock $\\tau^2$-bench: A benchmark for tool-augmented agent evaluation.\n451\t\\newblock 2025.\n452\t\n453\t\\bibitem[Wasow et~al.(2005)]{wasow2005intuitions}\n454\tThomas Wasow, Amy Perfors, and David Beaver.\n455\t\\newblock The puzzle of ambiguity.\n456\t\\newblock In \\emph{Morphology and the Web of Grammar: Essays in Memory of Steven G.\\ Lapointe}, 2005.\n457\t\n458\t\\end{thebibliography}\n459\t\n460\t%==============================================================================\n461\t\\appendix\n462\t\\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy Cross-Walk}\n463\t\\label{app:crosswalk}\n464\t\n465\tTable~\\ref{tab:crosswalk} maps our 6-type taxonomy to three prior frameworks.\n466\t\n467\t\\begin{table}[h]\n468\t\\centering\n469\t\\small\n470\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}\n471\t\\toprule\n472\t\\textbf{Ours} & \\textbf{Li et al.} & \\textbf{Massey} & \\textbf{DRIP-R} \\\\\n473\t\\midrule\n474\tScopal & Scope, Attachment & Referential & Structural \\\\\n475\tCoreferential & Anaphoric & Referential & Referential \\\\\n476\tLexical & Lexical, Polysemy & Lexical & Lexical \\\\\n477\tAuth.\\ Scope & --- & Scope & --- \\\\\n478\tIncompleteness & Underspec. & Incompleteness & Vagueness \\\\\n479\tCond.\\ Prec. & Coordination & --- & Conflict \\\\\n480\t\\bottomrule\n481\t\\end{tabular}\n482\t\\caption{Cross-walk of our taxonomy with three prior frameworks. ``---'' indicates no direct correspondence.}\n483\t\\label{tab:crosswalk}\n484\t\\end{table}\n485\t\n486\t\\section{Clause Generation Details}\n487\t\\label{app:clause_gen}\n488\t\n489\tTable~\\ref{tab:clause_gen} summarizes the clause generation pipeline output.\n490\tAll 300 clauses pass Chantree-style quality audit at a 94\\% first-pass rate.\n491\tCross-type linguistic properties (length, parse depth, Flesch-Kincaid, TTR) show no significant differences (KS $p > 0.3$ for all pairwise comparisons).\n492\t\n493\t\\begin{table}[h]\n494\t\\centering\n495\t\\small\n496\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n497\t\\toprule\n498\t\\textbf{Type} & $n$ & \\textbf{New} & \\textbf{Len} & \\textbf{FK} & \\textbf{Pass} \\\\\n499\t\\midrule\n500\tScopal         & 50 & 0  & 27.8 & 11.0 & 96\\% \\\\\n501\tCoreferential  & 50 & 50 & 28.5 & 11.4 & 92\\% \\\\\n502\tLexical        & 50 & 20 & 28.1 & 11.1 & 94\\% \\\\\n503\tAuth.\\ Scope   & 50 & 50 & 29.0 & 11.5 & 94\\% \\\\\n504\tIncompleteness & 50 & 20 & 28.0 & 11.0 & 96\\% \\\\\n505\tCond.\\ Prec.   & 50 & 0  & 28.4 & 11.2 & 92\\% \\\\\n506\t\\bottomrule\n507\t\\end{tabular}\n508\t\\caption{Clause generation summary. Len: mean length (tokens). FK: Flesch-Kincaid grade level. Pass: Chantree-style quality audit pass rate.}\n509\t\\label{tab:clause_gen}\n510\t\\end{table}\n511\t\n512\t\\section{Reliability Analysis}\n513\t\\label{app:reliability}\n514\t\n515\tTo address concerns about API sampling noise (RC8), we conduct a test-retest reliability study: 30 clauses (5 per type) $\\times$ 3 replicates $\\times$ 2 conditions $\\times$ 4 models = 720 episodes.\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[h]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\small\n520\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}\n521\t\\toprule\n522\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n523\t\\midrule\n524\tOverall ICC & 0.82 \\\\\n525\tICC (GPT-5.4) & 0.85 \\\\\n526\tICC (GPT-4.1) & 0.80 \\\\\n527\tICC (Qwen3-32B) & 0.83 \\\\\n528\tICC (Qwen3-8B) & 0.78 \\\\\n529\tBootstrap CI width (mean) & 7.2$\\pp$ \\\\\n530\tBootstrap CI width (max) & 9.8$\\pp$ \\\\\n531\tMax replicate deviation & 6.4$\\pp$ \\\\\n532\t\\bottomrule\n533\t\\end{tabular}\n534\t\\caption{Test-retest reliability. ICC = 0.82 (``good'' reliability). The binary effect ($+33.6\\pp$) exceeds the noise band (6.4$\\pp$) by a factor of 5.3$\\times$.}\n535\t\\label{tab:reliability}\n536\t\\end{table}\n537\t\n538\t\\section{Realism Validation}\n539\t\\label{app:realism}\n540\t\n541\tWe compare our synthetic clauses against natural ambiguities from the DRIP-R corpus on four linguistic properties (Table~\\ref{tab:realism}).\n542\tA DeBERTa discriminator trained to distinguish synthetic from natural achieves only 58\\% accuracy (50\\% chance baseline).\n543\t\n544\t\\begin{table}[h]\n545\t\\centering\n546\t\\small\n547\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n548\t\\toprule\n549\t\\textbf{Property} & \\textbf{Synth.} & \\textbf{Natural} & \\textbf{KS $D$} & $p$ \\\\\n550\t\\midrule\n551\tSent.\\ length   & 28.3 & 31.7 & 0.14 & 0.22 \\\\\n552\tParse depth      & 5.8  & 6.3  & 0.11 & 0.38 \\\\\n553\tFlesch-Kincaid   & 11.2 & 10.6 & 0.09 & 0.51 \\\\\n554\tTTR              & 0.72 & 0.69 & 0.12 & 0.31 \\\\\n555\t\\bottomrule\n556\t\\end{tabular}\n557\t\\caption{Realism validation. No significant distributional differences between synthetic and natural ambiguities ($p > 0.22$ for all properties).}\n558\t\\label{tab:realism}\n559\t\\end{table}\n560\t\n561\t\\end{document}\n562\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erxdqjv2CBCMue1UZUJgBX","session_id":"759fb270-c694-4a1e-95d0-1940781c5183","uuid":"8ae30ffe-c79f-4406-98ea-a6e771fa0c39","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:20:44.155Z"}
