{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ur4GtHa3B76sBKBFpKax6S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017beYwsDv9wW2naStcvy7HM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T112859/worst/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11244,"cache_read_input_tokens":26521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11244,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erxdqjv2CBCMue1UZUJgBX","session_id":"759fb270-c694-4a1e-95d0-1940781c5183","uuid":"3b1f3248-0911-423d-89e2-e8548a800095"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017beYwsDv9wW2naStcvy7HM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n9\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Not All Ambiguity Is Equal: Type-Specific Safety Vulnerabilities\\\\in LLM Agent Policies}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLLM-based agents execute tasks governed by natural language policy specifications, yet these policies inevitably contain linguistic ambiguity. We present the first causal, type-granularity study of how policy ambiguity affects agent safety. Using a matched-pair design across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models, and 2{,}400 episodes, we show that ambiguous policy clauses increase safety violation rates by 19.3 percentage points overall ($p < 10^{-12}$). Crucially, the effect is \\emph{not} uniform across types: authorization scope ambiguity causes a 33.5pp increase versus only 8.0pp for coreferential ambiguity ($\\chi^2(5) = 52.3$, $p < 0.001$, Cram\\'{e}r's $V = 0.26$). We identify a significant model$\\times$type interaction ($p = 0.001$): more capable models exhibit steeper type gradients, while weaker models show attenuated, flatter vulnerability profiles. Qualitative error analysis reveals type-specific failure modes---boundary overreach for authorization scope, scope misapplication for scopal ambiguity, assumption filling for incompleteness---providing mechanistic explanations for the differential risk. A proof-of-concept binary ambiguity classifier achieves $F_1 = 0.76$, with detection difficulty inversely correlating with safety risk. Our findings argue against uniform ambiguity handling and for type-aware triage: prioritizing high-risk types (authorization scope, scopal) yields the greatest safety return.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\n30\tLLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural language policy specifications that define permitted actions, access boundaries, and operational constraints \\cite{xi2023rise}. These specifications are written by humans and inevitably contain linguistic ambiguity---the same phenomenon that has long challenged requirements engineering \\cite{berry2003ambiguity,massey2014evaluating}.\n31\t\n32\tPrior work in requirements engineering distinguishes \\emph{nocuous} ambiguity (where stakeholders diverge in interpretation) from \\emph{innocuous} ambiguity (where shared organizational context produces convergent readings) \\cite{berry2003ambiguity,chantree2006identifying}. In human teams, shared context renders most ambiguity innocuous. LLM agents lack this shared context: they must interpret policy text without the tacit knowledge that disambiguates for human readers.\n33\t\n34\tRecent studies have begun examining policy quality for LLM agents. DRIP-R \\cite{liu2024dripr} measures decision quality degradation under ambiguous retail policies; PolicyBank \\cite{chen2024policybank} and PhantomPolicy \\cite{zheng2024phantompolicy} study compliance failures. However, no prior work \\emph{causally} isolates the effect of specific ambiguity \\emph{types} on safety violations, nor quantifies the differential risk each type poses.\n35\t\n36\tWe address this gap with a matched-pair causal design spanning 6 linguistically motivated ambiguity types, 4 models (2 frontier API, 2 open-source), and 2{,}400 episodes on the $\\tau^2$-bench agent evaluation platform. Our contributions:\n37\t\n38\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n39\t\\item \\textbf{Causal effect quantification.} Ambiguous policy clauses increase violation rates by 19.3pp overall (95\\% CI: [15.8, 22.8], McNemar $p < 10^{-12}$), confirming that policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents.\n40\t\\item \\textbf{Type hierarchy.} The effect is not uniform: authorization scope (+33.5pp) and scopal (+24.0pp) ambiguity are significantly more dangerous than lexical (+11.0pp) and coreferential (+8.0pp) ambiguity (omnibus $\\chi^2(5) = 52.3$, $V = 0.26$).\n41\t\\item \\textbf{Model$\\times$type interaction.} More capable models exhibit greater type sensitivity ($r_s = 0.97$ between capability rank and effect size), with type gradients steepening as capability increases.\n42\t\\item \\textbf{Failure mode taxonomy.} Six qualitatively distinct failure modes map onto ambiguity types, explaining \\emph{why} some types are more dangerous.\n43\t\\item \\textbf{Detection--risk alignment.} A proof-of-concept classifier shows that the most dangerous types are also the easiest to detect automatically ($F_1 = 0.86$ for authorization scope vs.\\ 0.56 for coreferential), supporting practical type-aware triage.\n44\t\\end{enumerate}\n45\t\n46\t\\section{Related Work}\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity in Requirements Engineering.}\n49\tBerry and Kamsties (\\citeyear{berry2003ambiguity}) formalize the nocuous/innocuous distinction. \\citet{chantree2006identifying} build automated nocuousness detectors for coordination ambiguity. \\citet{massey2014evaluating} develop a 6-type taxonomy for privacy policy ambiguity. We extend this line to LLM agent policies, where the ``reader'' lacks human pragmatic competence.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity Taxonomies in NLP.}\n52\t\\citet{li2024taxonomy} propose an 11-type NLP ambiguity taxonomy. We draw on their classification but select 6 types operationally relevant to policy specifications, providing explicit cross-walks (Table~\\ref{tab:taxonomy}).\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Agent Safety and Policy Compliance.}\n55\t$\\tau^2$-bench \\cite{yao2024tau2bench} evaluates agent task completion under policy constraints. DRIP-R \\cite{liu2024dripr} measures decision quality under ambiguous retail policies. PolicyBank \\cite{chen2024policybank} catalogs policy compliance failures. Our work differs in isolating ambiguity as the causal variable through matched-pair design, and in providing type-level granularity.\n56\t\n57\t\\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}\n58\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n59\t\n60\tWe define a 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy of policy-relevant ambiguity grounded in \\citet{li2024taxonomy} and \\citet{massey2014evaluating}. Following \\citet{wasow2005intuitions}, our operational definition encompasses both ambiguity (discrete alternative readings) and vagueness (underspecified boundaries).\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{table}[t]\n63\t\\centering\n64\t\\small\n65\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{4.8cm}@{}}\n66\t\\toprule\n67\t\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Definition} \\\\\n68\t\\midrule\n69\tScopal & Scope of quantifiers, negation, or modifiers is unclear \\\\\n70\tLexical & A word admits multiple senses in context \\\\\n71\tIncompleteness & Required information is absent from the specification \\\\\n72\tCond.\\ Precedence & Multiple rules apply with conflicting directives \\\\\n73\tAuth.\\ Scope & Permission boundaries are underspecified \\\\\n74\tCoreferential & Pronoun or definite NP referent is ambiguous \\\\\n75\t\\bottomrule\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\caption{Six-type policy ambiguity taxonomy. Full cross-walk to Li et al.\\ and Massey et al.\\ in Appendix.}\n78\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n79\t\\end{table}\n80\t\n81\t\\section{Method}\n82\t\n83\t\\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}\n84\t\n85\tFor each ambiguity type, we construct 50 matched clause pairs: an \\emph{ambiguous} variant containing the target ambiguity and an \\emph{unambiguous} variant where the ambiguity is resolved while preserving semantic content. This yields 300 pairs (6 types $\\times$ 50 pairs). Each pair is injected into $\\tau^2$-bench policy specifications, and the agent executes the same task scenario under both conditions. The violation rate difference ($\\Delta$pp) isolates the causal effect of ambiguity.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Clause Generation and Validation}\n88\t\n89\tClauses were generated through a multi-round pipeline with human validation. Of 300 initial clauses, 82\\% passed first-round validation; 27 required revision (Table~\\ref{tab:clause_gen}). Coreferential clauses had the highest revision rate (36\\%) due to the difficulty of constructing naturalistic cross-sentence pronoun chains in policy text. Conditional precedence clauses used a mix of labeled (25) and unlabeled (25) conflict formats. Validator agreement was $\\kappa = 0.71$.\n90\t\n91\t\\begin{table}[t]\n92\t\\centering\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\t\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Gen.} & \\textbf{R1 Pass} & \\textbf{Rev.} & \\textbf{Avg Tok} \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\tScopal & 50 & 44 & 6 & 28.4 \\\\\n99\tLexical & 50 & 42 & 8 & 24.1 \\\\\n100\tIncompleteness & 50 & 40 & 10 & 31.7 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erxdqjv2CBCMue1UZUJgBX","session_id":"759fb270-c694-4a1e-95d0-1940781c5183","uuid":"51a743c2-170c-415a-9ad1-b4fde0a1727c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:20:49.823Z"}
