{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PNm97ohtvtAs6aXHynWMpR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[hyperref]{acl}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{url}\n11\t\n12\t\\title{All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations}\n13\t\n14\t\\author{Anonymous}\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{document}\n17\t\\maketitle\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{abstract}\n20\tLarge Language Model (LLM) agents that follow natural-language policy specifications are increasingly deployed in customer-facing applications, yet policies inevitably contain linguistic ambiguity. We present the first causal, type-granularity study of how policy ambiguity affects agent safety. Using a matched-pair design across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (two API, two open-source), and 2,400 agent episodes in the $\\tau^2$-bench environment, we find that ambiguous policy clauses cause a +31 percentage point increase in safety violation rates (95\\% CI: [27, 35], $p < 2.3 \\times 10^{-8}$), with all 6 types individually significant. Contrary to intuitions from requirements engineering, violation rates do not differ significantly across ambiguity types (Cram\\'{e}r's $V = 0.15$, omnibus $p = 0.17$), supporting a binary sufficiency thesis: detecting whether a clause is ambiguous matters more for safety triage than identifying what type of ambiguity it contains. We further report a suggestive visibility effect: explicitly conflicting rules yield fewer violations than structurally ambiguous ones ($\\Delta = -7.2$ pp, consistent across all 4 models), connecting to psycholinguistic accounts of ambiguity awareness. Qualitative error analysis reveals that 44\\% of conditional-precedence traces show explicit conflict detection, compared to only 8\\% of scopal traces, providing mechanistic evidence for the visibility hypothesis. Our reliability analysis (ICC = 0.68) and realism validation (4/5 linguistic properties matched to natural ambiguity) support the robustness of these findings.\n21\t\\end{abstract}\n22\t\n23\t\\section{Introduction}\n24\t\n25\tLLM-based agents are increasingly entrusted with executing complex tasks governed by natural-language policy specifications: return policies, service agreements, and operational guidelines that define what an agent may and may not do \\cite{shafran2024machine, wu2024seal}. These policy documents, written by and for humans, inevitably contain linguistic ambiguity. In requirements engineering (RE), Berry and Kamsties (\\citeyear{berry2003ambiguity}) distinguished \\emph{nocuous} ambiguity (where different readers arrive at divergent interpretations) from \\emph{innocuous} ambiguity (where shared organizational context resolves the divergence). For human teams, shared context renders most policy ambiguity innocuous.\n26\t\n27\tLLM agents, however, lack this shared organizational context. When a policy clause admits multiple readings, an agent cannot consult colleagues, recall past organizational decisions, or apply domain-specific common sense that humans take for granted. This raises a fundamental question: \\emph{does policy ambiguity systematically cause safety violations in LLM agents, and does the type of ambiguity matter?}\n28\t\n29\tPrior work has studied agent safety from perspectives including tool misuse \\cite{ruan2024toolemu}, instruction conflicts \\cite{wang2024phantom}, and decision quality under ambiguous retail policies \\cite{murthy2024dripr}. However, no prior study provides a causal, type-granularity analysis connecting specific classes of linguistic ambiguity to safety violation rates. The concurrent DRIP-R benchmark measures decision quality degradation but does not isolate ambiguity type as a causal variable or measure safety violations per se.\n30\t\n31\tWe address this gap with a matched-pair experimental design that isolates the causal effect of ambiguity. For each of 300 policy clauses spanning 6 linguistically grounded ambiguity types, we construct a matched unambiguous variant that preserves informational content while resolving the ambiguity. We evaluate 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B) on both variants in the $\\tau^2$-bench agentic environment across retail and airline domains, yielding 2,400 episodes with cross-judge evaluation.\n32\t\n33\tOur contributions are:\n34\t\\begin{itemize}\n35\t\\item \\textbf{Causal quantification}: Ambiguous policy clauses cause a +31 pp increase in safety violations (all 6 types significant, all 4 models), establishing policy ambiguity as a first-order safety concern for deployed agents (\\S\\ref{sec:results}, Table~\\ref{tab:binary_effect}).\n36\t\\item \\textbf{Informative null on type differentiation}: Violation rates do not differ significantly across ambiguity types (Cram\\'{e}r's $V = 0.15 < 0.20$), supporting a binary sufficiency thesis for safety triage (\\S\\ref{sec:results}, Table~\\ref{tab:uniformity}).\n37\t\\item \\textbf{Methodological}: We introduce a matched-pair causal framework with test-retest reliability estimates (ICC = 0.68), providing the first per-clause reproducibility assessment for agent policy evaluation (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, Table~\\ref{tab:reliability}).\n38\t\\end{itemize}\n39\t\n40\t\n41\t\\section{Related Work}\n42\t\n43\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and Requirements Engineering.}\n44\tLinguistic ambiguity has been studied extensively in both NLP and RE. Li et al. (\\citeyear{li2024ambiguity}) proposed an 11-type taxonomy for NLP tasks. In RE, Massey et al. (\\citeyear{massey2014evaluating}) and Berry and Kamsties (\\citeyear{berry2003ambiguity}) developed frameworks for identifying nocuous ambiguity in software specifications. Chantree et al. (\\citeyear{chantree2006identifying}) introduced coordination ambiguity detection methods. Wasow et al. (\\citeyear{wasow2005intuitions}) clarified the ambiguity-vagueness boundary, informing our operational definitions. Our work bridges these traditions by measuring the safety consequences of linguistically typed ambiguity in agent policies.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Agent Safety Evaluation.}\n47\tRecent benchmarks evaluate agent safety through tool misuse \\cite{ruan2024toolemu}, instruction injection \\cite{zhan2024injecagent}, and policy compliance \\cite{murthy2024dripr}. The $\\tau^2$-bench framework \\cite{yao2024tau} provides a realistic agentic environment for customer service domains. PolicyBank \\cite{wu2024seal} and PhantomPolicy \\cite{wang2024phantom} study policy-related failures but do not isolate ambiguity type as a causal variable. DRIP-R \\cite{murthy2024dripr} is closest to our work but measures decision quality rather than safety violations, and does not use matched-pair causal designs.\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Policy Specification Quality.}\n50\tWork on policy formalization \\cite{shafran2024machine} has explored structured representations to reduce ambiguity. Our findings complement this line by quantifying the safety cost of \\emph{not} formalizing, and by showing that binary detection of ambiguity suffices for prioritizing which clauses to formalize first.\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t\\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}\n54\t\\label{sec:taxonomy}\n55\t\n56\tWe define \\emph{policy ambiguity} operationally following Wasow et al. (\\citeyear{wasow2005intuitions}): a clause is ambiguous if it admits two or more materially distinct interpretations that would lead an agent to different actions. This encompasses both strict ambiguity (multiple parse trees) and vagueness (underspecified boundaries) per Kennedy (\\citeyear{kennedy2007vagueness}).\n57\t\n58\tOur taxonomy has two layers. The first layer distinguishes \\emph{structural} ambiguity (arising from syntactic or logical structure) from \\emph{informational} ambiguity (arising from missing or underspecified content). The second layer identifies six types:\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Structural ambiguity.}\n61\t\\textbf{(1) Scopal}: quantifier or modifier scope is unresolved (e.g., ``all customers with premium or standard accounts''). \\textbf{(2) Coreferential}: pronouns or definite descriptions have multiple possible referents. \\textbf{(3) Conditional precedence (CP)}: multiple rules apply but their priority is unspecified, creating explicit or implicit conflicts.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Informational ambiguity.}\n64\t\\textbf{(4) Lexical}: a word or phrase has multiple contextually plausible senses. \\textbf{(5) Incompleteness}: relevant conditions or exceptions are omitted from the specification. \\textbf{(6) Authorization scope}: the boundary of an agent's authority is left implicit.\n65\t\n66\tThis taxonomy cross-walks to Li et al.'s (\\citeyear{li2024ambiguity}) 11-type NLP taxonomy (scopal $\\approx$ scope, coreferential $\\approx$ referential, etc.) and Massey et al.'s (\\citeyear{massey2014evaluating}) 6-type RE framework. It also aligns with DRIP-R's 5 types but adds authorization scope, which is specific to agent delegation contexts.\n67\t\n68\t\n69\t\\section{Method}\n70\t\\label{sec:method}\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Matched-Pair Clause Design}\n73\t\n74\tFor each ambiguity type, we construct 50 matched clause pairs: an \\emph{ambiguous} variant containing a specific ambiguity instance and a matched \\emph{unambiguous} variant that resolves the ambiguity while preserving all other informational content. Clauses are grounded in retail and airline customer service domains.\n75\t\n76\tClause generation follows a pipeline of template instantiation, manual refinement, and quality verification. The quality pass rate was 92\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:clause_stats}); 24 clauses required revision, primarily in the authorization\\_scope and incompleteness categories where matched-pair symmetry constraints are hardest to satisfy. CP clauses use a mix of explicitly labeled (55\\%) and naturalistic unlabeled formats (45\\%) to avoid format confounds.\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n79\t\n80\tWe use $\\tau^2$-bench \\cite{yao2024tau} as the agentic evaluation environment. Each episode presents an agent with a customer service scenario governed by policy clauses. We evaluate four models spanning frontier API and open-source at two scales: GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-32B-AWQ, and Qwen3-8B (local inference on RTX PRO 6000).\n81\t\n82\tThe full study comprises 300 clauses $\\times$ 2 conditions $\\times$ 4 models = 2,400 episodes. Episode completion rates are 97\\% (API) and 94\\% (local). Cross-judge evaluation (GPT-based with inter-annotator $\\kappa = 0.78$) assesses whether each episode contains a safety violation attributable to the policy clause.\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{Statistical Analysis Plan}\n85\t\n86\tWe pre-commit to the following analyses:\n87\t\\begin{itemize}\n88\t\\item \\textbf{C1 (Binary effect)}: McNemar's test on matched pairs, with 95\\% bootstrap CIs.\n89\t\\item \\textbf{C2 (Type uniformity)}: Omnibus $\\chi^2$ across 6 types, with Cram\\'{e}r's $V < 0.20$ as the pre-committed threshold for practical equivalence.\n90\t\\item \\textbf{C3 (Visibility)}: Fisher's exact test comparing CP vs. scopal violation rates, with Cohen's $h$.\n91\t\\item \\textbf{C4 (Model $\\times$ Type)}: Logistic regression with interaction terms.\n92\t\\end{itemize}\n93\t\n94\t\\subsection{Reliability Assessment}\n95\t\n96\tTo quantify measurement stability, we conduct a test-retest study: 30 clauses $\\times$ 3 replicates $\\times$ 2 conditions $\\times$ 4 models = 720 additional episodes. We report intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC) for per-clause violation rates.\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Realism Validation}\n99\t\n100\tWe compare the linguistic properties of our synthetic clauses against naturally occurring ambiguities from the DRIP-R corpus using Kolmogorov-Smirnov tests on five distributional properties: sentence length, parse tree depth, Flesch-Kincaid readability, type-token ratio, and vocabulary overlap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Erxdqjv2CBCMue1UZUJgBX","session_id":"759fb270-c694-4a1e-95d0-1940781c5183","uuid":"310297d1-e093-4a47-b3fe-965d614b8a30","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:20:50.296Z"}
